        "completedSourceTexts": [
          {
            "textId": "completed-block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:seven-choices:1",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:seven-choices:1",
            "text": "One of the most common mistakes in communication is trying to do too much. The best messages are often simple. There’s no value in delivering any kind of communication, whether written, spoken, formal, or informal, if the message doesn’t come across clearly. Being clear and brief – while maintaining interest and including everything your listeners need to know – is a high-level communication skill."
          },
          {
            "textId": "completed-block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:seven-choices:4",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:seven-choices:4",
            "text": "Your audience will naturally be more interested and engaged when you tailor your communications to their interests. Speak directly to what matters to them. This will engage their desire to understand and interact with the information."
          },
          {
            "textId": "completed-block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:seven-choices:6",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:seven-choices:6",
            "text": "Even the most engaged and committed audience will eventually get bored. Keeping your message simple and clear will make it easier for people to understand. As the speaker, you already know what you’re going to say. However, they’re hearing it for the first time. Keep it simple."
          },
          {
            "textId": "completed-block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:seven-choices:7",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:seven-choices:7",
            "text": "● Get the audience involved."
          },
          {
            "textId": "completed-block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:seven-choices:10",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-gaokao-2025-zhejiang-jan:seven-choices:10",
            "text": "If the information you’re communicating isn’t urgent, consider sending an email. Written communication will give your audience more time to review it, think it over, and follow up with questions. It will also give them a handy record to refer back to."
          }
        ],
